surface; concentrated beds form 'shell hash' limestone."}, {"id": "fg-petrified-wood", "name": "Petrified (silicified) wood", "aliases": ["petrified wood", "silicified wood", "fossil wood", "palmwood", "Palmoxylon"], "typology_system": "Fossil group (plant)", "category": "fossil", "period": "Devonian-Neogene", "dates": "~390-2.6 Ma; formations: Wilcox (64.92-33.9 Ma), Jackson (37.71-10.08 Ma)", "continent": "Worldwide", "country": [], "admin1": ["Texas"], "region_notes": "Worldwide group; Texas formations listed where known", "traits": ["wood grain", "growth rings", "bark", "fibrous", "silicified", "checking", "knots"], "similar_types": ["banded chalcedony/agate", "fibrous gypsum", "weathered bedded sandstone"], "texas": true, "home_priority": 1, "sources": [{"name": "Paleobiology Database (PBDB)", "url": "https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Plantae", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "National Park Service, Fossils & Paleontology", "url": "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/fossils/index.htm", "license": "Public domain (US Government work)", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Wikidata", "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1149633", "license": "CC0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Wilcox", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Jackson", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}], "rules": true, "group_id": null, "fossil_kind": "plant", "age_ma": [390, 2.6], "formations": ["Catahoula (Texas state stone)", "Wilcox", "Jackson", "Nevada Miocene (e.g. Virgin Valley, Hubbard Basin)"], "lookalikes": ["banded chalcedony/agate", "fibrous gypsum", "weathered bedded sandstone"], "localities": [], "taxonomy": null, "properties": null, "diagnostic": "Wood replaced by silica: lengthwise grain, end-grain growth rings and rays, shrinkage checks across the grain. Texas: Catahoula and other Paleogene-Neogene units. Nevada is famous for Miocene opalized and agatized wood."}, {"id": "pf-concretion", "name": "Pseudofossil: concretions, septarian nodules and iron nodules", "aliases": ["concretion", "septarian", "iron concretion", "mud ball", "pseudofossil"], "typology_system": "Pseudofossil (not a fossil)", "category": "fossil", "period": "Any", "dates": "any age; formations: Eagle Ford (102.66-88.78 Ma), Taylor (82.17-66.0 Ma), Wilcox (64.92-33.9 Ma)", "continent": "Worldwide", "country": [], "admin1": ["Texas"], "region_notes": "Worldwide group; Texas formations listed where known", "traits": ["round", "nodule", "concentric", "cracks", "knobby", "iron stained", "smooth"], "similar_types": ["eggs", "bones", "turtle shells", "sponges", "heads/teeth"], "texas": true, "home_priority": 1, "sources": [{"name": "USGS (public domain)", "url": "https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-are-fossils", "license": "Public domain (US Government work)", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Wikidata", "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1141017", "license": "CC0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Eagle Ford", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Taylor", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}], "rules": true, "group_id": null, "fossil_kind": "pseudo", "age_ma": null, "formations": ["Eagle Ford", "Taylor", "Wilcox"], "lookalikes": ["eggs", "bones", "turtle shells", "sponges", "heads/teeth"], "localities": [], "taxonomy": null, "properties": null, "diagnostic": "Hard masses cemented around a nucleus, often round or knobby, sometimes with crack fillings (septarian). Lack internal anatomy (no bone texture, no septa, no growth lines). The most common 'fossil' lookalike."}, {"id": "pf-dendrite", "name": "Pseudofossil: manganese dendrites", "aliases": ["dendrite", "manganese dendrite", "pseudofossil"], "typology_system": "Pseudofossil (not a fossil)", "category": "fossil", "period": "Any", "dates": "mineral growth, any age", "continent": "Worldwide", "country": [], "admin1": ["Texas"], "region_notes": "Worldwide group; Texas formations listed where known", "traits": ["branching", "black", "fern-like", "surface film"], "similar_types": ["fern or plant fossils"], "texas": true, "home_priority": 1, "sources": [{"name": "USGS (public domain)", "url": "https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-are-fossils", "license": "Public domain (US Government work)", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Wikidata", "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1191045", "license": "CC0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}], "rules": true, "group_id": null, "fossil_kind": "pseudo", "age_ma": null, "formations": [], "lookalikes": ["fern or plant fossils"], "localities": [], "taxonomy": null, "properties": null, "diagnostic": "Black, fern-like mineral crystals (manganese oxides) on rock surfaces and fractures; branch pattern has no midrib or cell structure. Not a fossil."}, {"id": "pf-weathering", "name": "Pseudofossil: weathering pits, chert knobs and dissolution shapes", "aliases": ["weathering pits", "pseudofossil", "knobby limestone"], "typology_system": "Pseudofossil (not a fossil)", "category": "fossil", "period": "Any", "dates": "any age", "continent": "Worldwide", "country": [], "admin1": ["Texas"], "region_notes": "Worldwide group; Texas formations listed where known", "traits": ["pitted", "knobby", "holes", "irregular", "weathered"], "similar_types": ["sponges", "corals", "bone"], "texas": true, "home_priority": 1, "sources": [{"name": "USGS (public domain)", "url": "https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-are-fossils", "license": "Public domain (US Government work)", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}], "rules": true, "group_id": null, "fossil_kind": "pseudo", "age_ma": null, "formations": [], "lookalikes": ["sponges", "corals", "bone"], "localities": [], "taxonomy": null, "properties": null, "diagnostic": "Pits, cups and knobs left where softer parts weather out of limestone or chert; the pattern is random and lacks repeated anatomical structure."}, {"id": "fg-echinoid", "name": "Sea urchins (echinoids), incl. heart urchins", "aliases": ["echinoid", "sea urchin", "heart urchin", "Hemiaster", "Phymosoma", "sand dollar", "Macraster"], "typology_system": "Fossil group (body fossil)", "category": "fossil", "period": "Ordovician-Recent", "dates": "~450 Ma-present; formations: Glen Rose (113.2-100.5 Ma), Walnut (113.2-100.5 Ma), Del Rio (101.96-95.55 Ma), Austin (89.8-75.05 Ma)", "continent": "Worldwide", "country": [], "admin1": ["Texas"], "region_notes": "Worldwide group; Texas formations listed where known", "traits": ["five-part", "star pattern", "ambulacra", "test", "tubercles", "heart-shaped", "dome"], "similar_types": ["round concretions", "sponges"], "texas": true, "home_priority": 1, "sources": [{"name": "Paleobiology Database (PBDB)", "url": "https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Echinoidea", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "UT Bureau of Economic Geology, Geologic Atlas of Texas", "url": "https://www.beg.utexas.edu/research/programs/geologic-mapping", "license": "Facts only (summarized); cite BEG", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Glen Rose", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Walnut", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}], "rules": true, "group_id": null, "fossil_kind": "body", "age_ma": [450, 0], "formations": ["Glen Rose", "Walnut", "Del Rio", "Austin"], "lookalikes": ["round concretions", "sponges"], "localities": [], "taxonomy": null, "properties": null, "diagnostic": "Rounded test with five-fold (pentaradial) symmetry: five petal-like ambulacral bands; heart urchins have a groove at the front."}, {"id": "fg-shark", "name": "Shark and ray teeth", "aliases": ["shark tooth", "Cretoxyrhina", "Squalicorax", "Ptychodus", "Otodus", "Scapanorhynchus", "ray teeth"], "typology_system": "Fossil group (body fossil)", "category": "fossil", "period": "Devonian-Recent", "dates": "~400 Ma-present; formations: Eagle Ford (102.66-88.78 Ma), Austin (89.8-75.05 Ma), Kemp (67.55-67.05 Ma), Weches (42.79-40.48 Ma)", "continent": "Worldwide", "country": [], "admin1": ["Texas"], "region_notes": "Worldwide group; Texas formations listed where known", "traits": ["tooth", "enamel", "glossy", "root", "serrated", "triangular", "dark"], "similar_types": ["chert flakes and small points (no root, no enamel)"], "texas": true, "home_priority": 1, "sources": [{"name": "Paleobiology Database (PBDB)", "url": "https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Chondrichthyes", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "UT Bureau of Economic Geology, Geologic Atlas of Texas", "url": "https://www.beg.utexas.edu/research/programs/geologic-mapping", "license": "Facts only (summarized); cite BEG", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Eagle Ford", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Austin", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}], "rules": true, "group_id": null, "fossil_kind": "body", "age_ma": [400, 0], "formations": ["Eagle Ford", "Austin", "Kemp", "Weches"], "lookalikes": ["chert flakes and small points (no root, no enamel)"], "localities": [], "taxonomy": null, "properties": null, "diagnostic": "Glossy enamel crown on a two-lobed bony root; Ptychodus teeth are flat crushing plates with ridges. Abundant in Texas Cretaceous chalks and Eocene greensands."}, {"id": "fg-gastropod", "name": "Snails (gastropods), incl. Turritella", "aliases": ["gastropod", "snail", "Turritella", "Tylostoma", "Volutocorbis"], "typology_system": "Fossil group (body fossil)", "category": "fossil", "period": "Cambrian-Recent", "dates": "~520 Ma-present; formations: Weches (42.79-40.48 Ma), Glen Rose (113.2-100.5 Ma), Navarro (75.05-66.0 Ma)", "continent": "Worldwide", "country": [], "admin1": ["Texas"], "region_notes": "Worldwide group; Texas formations listed where known", "traits": ["spiral", "coiled", "whorls", "aperture", "high spire", "internal mold", "shell mold"], "similar_types": ["ammonites (have sutures)", "concretions with spiral cracks"], "texas": true, "home_priority": 1, "sources": [{"name": "Paleobiology Database (PBDB)", "url": "https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Gastropoda", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Weches", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Glen Rose", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}], "rules": true, "group_id": null, "fossil_kind": "body", "age_ma": [520, 0], "formations": ["Weches", "Glen Rose", "Navarro"], "lookalikes": ["ammonites (have sutures)", "concretions with spiral cracks"], "localities": [], "taxonomy": null, "properties": null, "diagnostic": "Single shell coiled in a helical (3-D) spiral around an axis, no internal chambers or suture lines; Turritella is tall and screw-like."}, {"id": "fg-sponge", "name": "Sponges (Porifera), incl. silicified sponges", "aliases": ["sponge", "Porocystis", "Siphonia", "hexactinellid"], "typology_system": "Fossil group (body fossil)", "category": "fossil", "period": "Cambrian-Recent", "dates": "~540 Ma-present; formations: Glen Rose (113.2-100.5 Ma), Austin (89.8-75.05 Ma)", "continent": "Worldwide", "country": [], "admin1": ["Texas"], "region_notes": "Worldwide group; Texas formations listed where known", "traits": ["porous", "canals", "cup", "knobby", "silicified", "osculum"], "similar_types": ["chert nodules", "weathered limestone", "concretions"], "texas": true, "home_priority": 1, "sources": [{"name": "Paleobiology Database (PBDB)", "url": "https://paleobiodb.org/classic/checkTaxonInfo?taxon_name=Porifera", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Glen Rose", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Austin", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}], "rules": true, "group_id": null, "fossil_kind": "body", "age_ma": [540, 0], "formations": ["Glen Rose", "Austin", "Cretaceous flints of Europe"], "lookalikes": ["chert nodules", "weathered limestone", "concretions"], "localities": [], "taxonomy": null, "properties": null, "diagnostic": "Cup, ball or vase shapes with pores, canals and one or more larger openings (oscula); no radial septa. Often silicified, which makes them easy to confuse with chert nodules."}, {"id": "fg-trace-burrow", "name": "Trace fossils: burrows (Thalassinoides, Ophiomorpha)", "aliases": ["burrow", "trace fossil", "Thalassinoides", "Ophiomorpha", "ichnofossil"], "typology_system": "Trace fossil", "category": "fossil", "period": "Cambrian-Recent", "dates": "~540 Ma-present; formations: Austin (89.8-75.05 Ma), Eagle Ford (102.66-88.78 Ma), Navarro (75.05-66.0 Ma)", "continent": "Worldwide", "country": [], "admin1": ["Texas"], "region_notes": "Worldwide group; Texas formations listed where known", "traits": ["burrow", "tubes", "branching", "knobby lining", "cylindrical"], "similar_types": ["root casts", "concretions", "sponges"], "texas": true, "home_priority": 1, "sources": [{"name": "USGS (public domain)", "url": "https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-are-fossils", "license": "Public domain (US Government work)", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "National Park Service, Fossils & Paleontology", "url": "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/fossils/index.htm", "license": "Public domain (US Government work)", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Austin", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}, {"name": "Macrostrat", "url": "https://macrostrat.org/lex/strat_names?name=Eagle Ford", "license": "CC BY 4.0", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}], "rules": true, "group_id": null, "fossil_kind": "trace", "age_ma": [540, 0], "formations": ["Austin", "Eagle Ford", "Navarro", "Cretaceous chalks of Europe"], "lookalikes": ["root casts", "concretions", "sponges"], "localities": [], "taxonomy": null, "properties": null, "diagnostic": "Cylindrical tubes or branching networks made by animals; Ophiomorpha tubes have a pelleted, knobby outer wall. Flint nodules in chalk often fill Thalassinoides burrows."}, {"id": "fg-trace-track", "name": "Trace fossils: tracks and trails (incl. dinosaur tracks)", "aliases": ["dinosaur track", "footprint", "trail", "Acrocanthosaurus track"], "typology_system": "Trace fossil", "category": "fossil", "period": "Cambrian-Recent", "dates": "~540 Ma-present", "continent": "Worldwide", "country": [], "admin1": ["Texas"], "region_notes": "Worldwide group; Texas formations listed where known", "traits": ["track", "footprint", "trail", "impression"], "similar_types": ["weathering pits", "potholes"], "texas": true, "home_priority": 1, "sources": [{"name": "National Park Service, Fossils & Paleontology", "url": "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/fossils/index.htm", "license": "Public domain (US Government work)", "retrieved": "2026-10-16"}], "rules": true, "group_id": null, "fossil_kind": "trace", "age_ma": [540, 0], "formations": ["Glen Rose (Dinosaur Valley State Park)"], "lookalikes": ["weathering pits", "potholes"], "localities": [], "taxonomy": null, "properties": null, "diagnostic": "Impressions left by moving animals; dinosaur tracks in the Glen Rose Formation are world-famous."}, {"id": "fg-trilobite", "name": "Trilobites", "aliases": ["trilobite", "Elrathia", "Phacops"], "typology_system": "Fossil g